WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.323
- Good afternoon, everyone, and welcome.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.260
- I'm Stephen Pitti, Professor
of History American Studies

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.430
and Ethnicity, Race in Migration.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.800
And I'm also the director of
the Yale Center for the Study

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.820
of Race, Indigeneity, and
Transnational Migration.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.880
It's wonderful to see so
many people here today

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:23.150
are with us for this
exciting conversation.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:25.030
We're happy to host this conversation

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:28.150
about the first English
language translation

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.560
of Severo Sarduy's poems.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:31.730
Keeping in mind, of course

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:35.210
that in talking about this XL Cuban author

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:38.019
those of us in new Havana are doing so

00:00:38.019 --> 00:00:40.580
on the historical and
contemporary homelands

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.690
of many indigenous people and nations

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:45.240
including the Mohegan
Mashantucket Pequot,

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.820
Eastern Pequot, Schaghticoke, 
Golden Hill Paugussett.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.610
Niantic, Quinnipiac and others.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:56.460
Our conversation today features
translator David Francis

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:57.740
who serves as Dean

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:01.040
of Grace Hopper College
here at Yale university

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.810
where he also teaches in
the program in ethnicity,

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.890
race and migration.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:09.700
David's translations and poems
have appeared in inventory.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.710
The FSG Book of Twentieth
Century Latin American Poetry,

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:18.710
Guernica, Exchanges, The
Brooklyn Rail, Poetry Daily,

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.683
The Chronicle of Higher
Education and elsewhere.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.720
Professor Francis holds
an M.F.A in poetry writing

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.470
from Columbia University

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:30.380
and a PhD in Romans,
Languages and Literature

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.250
from Harvard university.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.950
He received a Fulbright
to translate the poetry

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:39.420
of Colombian writer, Jose
Asuncion Silva into English.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.630
And he is currently translating
a collection of poems

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:45.810
by the Cuban author,
Jorge Olivera Castillo

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.013
and another by poets from Northern Mexico.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.130
As I already mentioned about
briefly, his translation

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.780
of Severo Surduy's poetry footwork

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:59.030
just published in 2021
by circumference books

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.710
is the first collection
of the Cuban authors,

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:03.893
poems translated into English.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:07.253
David Francis will be
in conversation today

00:02:07.253 --> 00:02:10.290
with Shawn McDaniel, a Terrific Scholar

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:13.628
who is currently assistant
professor of American studies

00:02:13.628 --> 00:02:17.833
and ethnicity at the University
of Southern California.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:21.670
Intersecting, Caribbean, Latin American,

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:24.050
and Latin X literary and cultural studies

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.990
and an intellectual history,

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:30.040
Professor McDaniel's research
explores, race, gender

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:34.340
sexuality, aesthetics,
modernism, and dissidents

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:38.960
in print, visual and Sonic
cultures at the Americas.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.290
His first book, centenary subjects

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:46.020
race reason and rupture in
the Americas is forthcoming

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:50.260
later this year from
Vanderbilt University Press.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.090
Professor McDaniel is
working on a second book

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:56.000
entitled Cuban Chic, Queer Deco,

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.570
and Diasphoric Modernism in New York,

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.297
which examines the literary,

00:03:00.297 --> 00:03:04.840
graphic, sartorial, sonic
and cinematic cultures

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:08.830
of cubanidad in New York

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:13.520
as well as its inter-American
routes through Havana,

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.520
Santiago, Marida,
Veracruz and Los Angeles.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:22.660
We are in the Yale
Center feel very honored

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.150
to be able to host this
celebration of David Francis

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:27.860
his translation of Sarduy's poems.

