00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:32.590
I want to start with a brief
introduction to who Sarduy is,

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:35.970
reading from the first page of the book.

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:39.370
And then what I'll do is
give a sort of Panorama

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:40.750
of poems that I translated

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:43.750
from the beginning of the work to the end.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.100
And that should take about 20 minutes.

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:50.890
And then I really can't
wait to, discuss the poems

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:54.360
and questions that everyone
has about the translations,

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:57.770
about Sarduy of the book
afterward with Shawn.

00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:00.003
So, that will be coming soon.

00:06:01.739 --> 00:06:05.210
The scarred body or the
image of its absence,

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:07.150
faced with the question
of how to translate

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:09.460
the body of Sarduy's poetry.

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:11.830
I feel a sense of stuttering.

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:14.760
How to convey the enormity
of seeming contradiction,

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.260
I encountered about
translating this poems,

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:20.470
indeed a body of work
that seems on its own

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:24.090
that celebrates the kind of
life essential experiences

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:28.490
of dance, of painting, food,
music, and sexual pleasure.

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:30.960
But that also recognizes
in these pleasures

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:32.783
the imminence of one's passing.

00:06:34.061 --> 00:06:37.770
And is 56 years of life,
the exiled poet, novelist,

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:41.120
essayist, painter and
world traveler managed

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:43.720
to include such a vast
range of perspectives

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:45.530
and poems in his texts.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:47.120
That in the process of selecting

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.580
which poems to include in Footwork,

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:52.980
I wanted to offer a survey
of his life as a poet,

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:54.310
from his earlier pieces

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:56.610
to those he composed
near the end of his life.

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:01.780
So, Sarduy was not known
to follow convention.

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:03.960
Nor did he think that
conventional approaches

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:06.740
to storytelling or lyrical composition

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:09.460
could capture the
complexities of human behavior

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:11.633
or personal and national identity.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:15.210
The publication of his
major works followed

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:17.896
the Cuban revolution and
the political turmoil

00:07:17.896 --> 00:07:20.660
that would ensue with the El Paso's rise

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:25.210
in Cuban dictator Fulgencio
Batista's loss of power in 1958.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:29.700
As boom literature
galvanized by such authors

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:34.310
as Mario Vargas Llosa, Julio
cork DASA in colors Wednesdays

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:36.680
brought Latin America
increasing visibility

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:39.720
in the world, literary stage.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:43.670
This gay author and artist
son of a railroad worker

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:45.760
redefined Latin American approaches

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:48.400
to the writing of literature
from the vantage point

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.130
of his exile in France
and from many other parts

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:52.550
of the world he visited

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:54.423
after departing his country of birth.

00:07:56.378 --> 00:07:59.530
Born in 1937 in Camaguey Cuba,

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:02.370
Sarduy started writing as a young man.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:04.700
Publishing in his teenage years in various

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:08.330
Cuban literary magazines
between Conaway and ranup.

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:11.570
After pursuing studies in
medicine in Cuba's Capital,

00:08:11.570 --> 00:08:14.920
he left the Island on
a scholarship in 1960

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:17.870
to study art criticism in Europe,

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:20.690
traveling to Madrid and then to Paris.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:23.540
Aware of Castro's efforts
to police homosexuality

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:26.580
and re-educate gay men
and work camps called

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:28.650
military units to aid production,

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:31.800
Sarduy would reside for the
rest of his life in Paris,

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:33.270
never to return home.

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:37.140
So do we achieve considerable notoriety

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:42.050
before his life was cut short
in 1993 to publications eight?

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:45.290
The author's first novel Gestos Gestures

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:48.190
was published in 1963.

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:50.480
But the book by which
he is most recognized

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:54.683
is the novel De donde son los
cantantes published in 1967.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:59.330
An experimental work of fiction
that Suzanne Jill Levine

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:02.280
translated definitely
into English as from Cuba

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:04.433
with a song in 1972.

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:08.720
Internationally celebrated for his work,

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:12.330
Sarduy won Francis
prestigious Prix Medicis prize

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:15.343
for his novel Cobra,
which came out in 1972.

00:09:16.637 --> 00:09:20.730
Sarduy published extensively
on his novels and essays

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:23.023
and continue to write about his poetry.

00:09:24.551 --> 00:09:27.820
Sarduy works contained
often chaotic convergence

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:30.610
of linguistic registers and characters

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:34.140
whose bodies just in
drug, tattooed or scarred,

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:37.950
distort, reconfigure or explode

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:40.670
in Big Bang fashion or in little deaths,

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:42.570
bourgeois efforts to codify

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:46.650
or define a singular
Latin American identity.

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:49.010
And what ensues I wish to share a series

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:51.020
of poems from his earliest works

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:53.930
to those he wrote near
the end of his life.

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:56.950
In doing so in preparation
for my conversation

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:58.730
with professor McDaniel,

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:01.060
I hope to illustrate not only some of

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:03.520
Sarduy's masterful formal renderings,

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.460
but also the expansiveness

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:07.650
with which he wrote into his works

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:10.980
fast geographies of art, philosophy,

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:13.817
science, desire and
determinations on death

00:10:13.817 --> 00:10:15.293
and the life of writing.

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:22.100
Now, I think I will be
sharing a visualization

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:26.130
of the poems I will be
reading just so that everyone

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:30.870
can get a sense not only of the language

00:10:32.892 --> 00:10:36.593
that Sarduy is using, but
also the brilliant ways

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:40.675
in which he plays with poem on the page,

00:10:40.675 --> 00:10:43.790
which is much more difficult to convey

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:45.790
just by reading his poems out loud.

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:48.860
So, I'll begin with a poem of Sevillanasa.

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:53.860
It's from an earlier
Chatbook type Flamenco,

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:58.830
since its first Chatbook
that came out in 1969

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:02.280
but it was republished inside his book

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:04.063
called Big Bang in 1974.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:09.510
And there's a series of poems in this book

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:13.030
dedicated to different forms
of Flamenco (indistinct).

00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:17.220
So, this form of dance that
we'll be reading about here

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:18.470
is called the Sevillanas.

00:11:19.339 --> 00:11:20.780
Sevillanas.

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:24.370
In the white SPACE, where
shadows are unrolled,

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:26.540
light goes gnawing at the borders,

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:30.140
folding the colors, destroying the forms,

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:32.960
IN THE WHITE SPACE, crossing over from

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:36.510
the other side of the
band of light, bursting in

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:38.942
without limits (without shadows)

00:11:38.942 --> 00:11:40.240
sphere

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:43.150
yellow rectangle, green stains

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:46.010
triangle, convergence of the iris

00:11:46.010 --> 00:11:49.233
pieces whose marble is
hardly visible are drawn,

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:54.930
are won over once more by the
light, decomposed, expelled

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:59.500
from the page, disjointed
figure, extinguished, that

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:01.990
IN THE WHITE SPACE, an instant later

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:06.240
goes insinuating its forms,
creating fringes of color,

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:09.960
defining their borders, appearing
while the excessive light

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:11.130
withdraws:

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:12.010
a head,

00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:15.240
a squared saffron, blue and green ovals,

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:16.150
a prism,

00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:17.690
a segmented body.

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:18.523
White.

00:12:21.982 --> 00:12:25.720
So I will move forward to another chatbook

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:30.720
that came out originally
in 1970 MOOD INDIGO

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:36.610
which follows Duke Ellington's
epidemic album called

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:41.013
mood Indigo and read a
poem within that book.

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:45.790
It's also published in the
re-publication of the Big Bang.

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:47.840
But this poem is called Echoes of Harlem.

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:53.170
Echoes of Harlem black Panthers

00:12:53.170 --> 00:12:54.990
with shut shutters

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:57.300
with planks standing doors,

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:00.030
with bags of sand and broken mirrors,

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:02.490
and amulets locking windows,

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:04.060
incantations, murderwhite

00:13:05.162 --> 00:13:07.253
Jean Genet in a forgotten suitcase,

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:09.880
machine guns

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:12.370
everything that must be broken

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:14.490
Echoes of striped Harlem

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:15.700
Cyclone,

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:17.460
little hash cakes,

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:18.670
Zero

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:20.740
Nina at the piano.

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:23.390
Everything must be destroyed,

00:13:23.390 --> 00:13:25.483
the next time fire.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:31.530
So as we move forward, I
won't be reading this poem

00:13:31.530 --> 00:13:33.540
but just to offer again,

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:35.730
another sense of how much Sarduy

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:39.523
in his earlier works is playing with form.

00:13:40.729 --> 00:13:44.980
I show you here Hong Kong Indigo,

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:49.800
and it's taking the shape of a spiral

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:53.400
and referring to a
number of musical venues

00:13:54.843 --> 00:13:57.050
in the history of Jazz music

00:13:58.330 --> 00:14:02.230
played in different
venues Transatlantically.

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:05.440
But I would like to
move on to the next call

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:07.900
that comes from Big Bang

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:13.110
which is essentially about Sarduy's,

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:16.610
obsessive thought about
the theory of the Big Bang

00:14:16.610 --> 00:14:18.210
as the creation of the universe

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:22.663
but also as sort of this staddling

00:14:22.663 --> 00:14:27.430
this figure standing in
for, or erotic desire

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:32.377
or the encounter to lovers
and many other things.

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:38.920
So much of Sarduy's, celestial
knowledge is encapsulated

00:14:39.340 --> 00:14:43.150
in these beautiful poems
about celestial bodies

00:14:43.150 --> 00:14:46.440
and heavenly bothered me is another term

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:49.500
to discuss one of these ideas

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:50.570
Heavenly Body

00:14:51.718 --> 00:14:54.960
The weight of your body on my body

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:57.530
saturing skin ciphered

00:14:57.530 --> 00:15:00.130
Green saliva on your back

00:15:00.130 --> 00:15:01.970
spine on spine,

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:03.370
knees knotted

00:15:03.370 --> 00:15:06.140
speared with phosphorescent lacquer

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:07.230
the bones

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:09.740
light up the black walled room

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:11.726
volumes articulating

00:15:11.726 --> 00:15:13.020
I'embottoast

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:14.960
entering in silence

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:17.290
greased, slowly

00:15:17.290 --> 00:15:18.920
each in another

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:20.580
each in another

00:15:20.580 --> 00:15:22.510
radiance, descending

00:15:22.510 --> 00:15:25.720
over the wall along the
distance of the wall

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:27.560
dead asters falling

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:29.150
to the sheets marble

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:36.435
This Palm concludes the
translation of Big Bang

00:15:36.435 --> 00:15:41.435
and really much of
Sarduy's experimental work

00:15:42.342 --> 00:15:43.730
with form.

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:47.810
But it doesn't conclude
his brilliant renderings

00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:51.407
of new sorts of language that

00:15:53.350 --> 00:15:55.150
goes into very traditional form

00:15:55.150 --> 00:15:57.550
and in a fleeting and masked witness

00:15:57.550 --> 00:15:59.383
which came out in 1985.

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:06.020
We see a series of
really beautiful sonnets,

00:16:06.050 --> 00:16:11.050
heavily evocative of
homoerotic desire and sex.

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:15.850
And so one of these poems I'd like to read

00:16:15.850 --> 00:16:20.480
they're all untitled, but
opening up with this book

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:24.380
is one of these beautiful
sonnets with the first one

00:16:24.380 --> 00:16:26.480
called the Murmur of Machine's was rowing.

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:30.350
The murmur of machines was growing

00:16:30.350 --> 00:16:33.350
in the adjacent room already; my hope

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:36.160
for an adjective or for your body

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:39.163
was nothing more than an
attempt to shorten the day.

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:42.183
The night was arriving and proceeding

00:16:42.183 --> 00:16:43.916
the desert wind, the certain

00:16:43.916 --> 00:16:47.410
light... or your naked feet on the mat...

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:50.023
of sunset, was suspending time.

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:54.570
I do not remember love only desire;

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:57.720
not the lack of faith, only the sphere...

00:16:57.720 --> 00:16:59.965
the image facing its reflection

00:16:59.965 --> 00:17:02.720
with white texture, the true

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:06.470
page _or your body, which I still recread

00:17:06.470 --> 00:17:08.553
vast, idiogram of spring.

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:16.820
Another example of
Sarduy's, obsessive work

00:17:16.820 --> 00:17:18.950
with not only the sonnet form

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:22.843
but with sound with
this subsequence on it.

00:17:24.670 --> 00:17:27.410
Not the voice proceded by the echo,

00:17:27.410 --> 00:17:30.230
nor the ravenous reflections
of the naked bodies

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:33.180
in the quicksilver of the quiet crystals,

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:35.903
but this simple brushstroke, dry:

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:39.963
the fruits on the table
and the colonial landscape.

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:43.400
When the hour of the siesta was

00:17:46.921 --> 00:17:50.110
enveloped us in the density
of itself swelling seas,

00:17:50.110 --> 00:17:52.930
or in the murmur of it's ended fiesta,

00:17:52.930 --> 00:17:56.590
When one in the other extinguished thirst,

00:17:56.590 --> 00:17:57.970
when light advanced

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:01.050
through the orchard that
the flame tree reddened,

00:18:01.050 --> 00:18:03.420
we opened then the great door

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:05.890
to the insular murmur of noon

00:18:05.890 --> 00:18:07.840
and to the timely still life.

00:18:12.504 --> 00:18:17.400
The final sonnet I'll be
reading from this section.

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:20.660
Pull out of me more
than what was left out;

00:18:20.660 --> 00:18:23.660
for when reaching and defining aporia

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:26.210
a planet on the other side its day

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:28.583
ignites the sight of its senses.

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:32.352
Not by force, for the humid temple

00:18:32.352 --> 00:18:35.680
(of Venus, the second)
does not take pleasure

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:39.393
in being wounded by the
Bersinian arrow or by frenzy.

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:44.270
Someday you will lay lubricants
or natural substances

00:18:44.270 --> 00:18:47.213
between the borders with cunning prudence,

00:18:48.200 --> 00:18:50.370
or with salivated caution

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:52.570
that might ease the
burning of your entrance

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:55.860
because with love and with ardor

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:59.083
in the annals of history
our naturals are ciphered.

00:19:05.133 --> 00:19:07.740
Nearing the end of Severo Sarduy's life,

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:11.230
we see another publication of sonnets

00:19:12.270 --> 00:19:14.320
which I've translated as the title of

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:18.960
a PERENNIAL AND BETRAYED
WITNESS that comes out in 1993.

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:21.230
But I'd like to focus in this section

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:23.173
of the reading or the conversation.

00:19:24.940 --> 00:19:26.950
On Sarduy's(indistinct) with DECIMAS

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:31.950
which different from the
side is an even more compact

00:19:34.026 --> 00:19:37.870
poem of 10 lines and a very strict drawing

00:19:37.870 --> 00:19:41.640
which was not reproducible in Spanish.

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:45.110
But in my work, in renderings

00:19:45.110 --> 00:19:46.370
is that DESIMAS into English.

00:19:46.370 --> 00:19:48.930
I did do my best to convey

00:19:48.930 --> 00:19:53.110
the sense of compactness and musicality.

00:19:53.110 --> 00:19:55.770
So the first series of DECIMAS is,

00:19:55.770 --> 00:19:59.383
comes from a series of poems
called the Crown of Fruits.

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:02.150
Cherimoya

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:04.030
Who hasn't tried a cherimoya

00:20:04.030 --> 00:20:07.080
in the shade of a Red Manjack tree?

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:12.080
Denae weaves and undoes
time in gold and rum,

00:20:12.410 --> 00:20:14.310
sickly sweet and sugary

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:16.373
for the inexperienced tongue;

00:20:17.420 --> 00:20:19.160
neither green nor ripe

00:20:19.160 --> 00:20:21.240
the palate takes delight.

00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:24.700
Fruit, no, hope of the fruit.

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:27.233
Pleasure: but pleasure standing still.

00:20:33.950 --> 00:20:37.880
I like to read now that
DECIMA number four,

00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:39.203
called Pineapple.

00:20:40.860 --> 00:20:44.210
I placed a bald pineapple
on three Chinese oranges,

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:46.783
and added in the corners
the sweetest guava.

00:20:47.692 --> 00:20:49.760
In this way, in exile,

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:52.640
the insular queen crowns, baroque,

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:55.840
her still life -little-

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:57.930
and death which I've offered her.

00:20:57.930 --> 00:21:00.090
And the emblem that it evokes.

00:21:00.090 --> 00:21:03.337
There will be no pain no
more pain or oblivios."

00:21:06.780 --> 00:21:08.170
Finally from the series,

00:21:08.170 --> 00:21:09.003
Cashew.

00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:12.450
However you close the mouth,

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:13.700
the tasty cashew,

00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:16.950
acidic and then sweet,

00:21:16.950 --> 00:21:19.610
turns your palate upside down.

00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:22.510
If you spill the juice
is of little matter.

00:21:22.510 --> 00:21:24.570
A sudden, idle drama,

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:26.300
a light fright

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:30.403
when its red hangs higher than
the hummingbird, than fire.

00:21:32.320 --> 00:21:34.760
The last DECIMA.

00:21:34.760 --> 00:21:37.340
I like to read from a
different series of DECIMA's,

00:21:37.340 --> 00:21:41.157
comes from a series dedicated to Orishas

00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:47.230
which Sarduy has dedicated
to Jorge and Miguel Barnet

00:21:48.410 --> 00:21:51.050
to THE Los Orishas En
Cuba the book written by

00:21:52.130 --> 00:21:54.097
Natalia Boliver Arostegui

00:21:54.097 --> 00:21:57.960
and of course to Lydia
Cabrera and Pierre Farsumbi.

00:21:57.960 --> 00:22:00.920
While I will be reading from this series

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:02.980
the final one titled Yernoja.

00:22:04.940 --> 00:22:09.050
Mother of water, new Moon, a dove, a lamb,

00:22:09.050 --> 00:22:12.000
I will offer to the austere sea,

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:13.943
that I might pass this test.

00:22:15.130 --> 00:22:17.100
With life comes death.

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:19.330
A cross of white vuscarilla,

00:22:19.330 --> 00:22:21.600
over the yellow forehead.

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:23.150
You will write my final breath.

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:26.750
And against tide and wind I'll row.

00:22:26.750 --> 00:22:28.283
Until I reach the other shore.

00:22:32.540 --> 00:22:35.750
Following Sarduy's death
came the publication

00:22:35.750 --> 00:22:38.733
in 1999 of his La era neobarraca.

00:22:41.010 --> 00:22:44.860
And this is one of the
most brilliant poems

00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:46.313
I think in his work,

00:22:48.620 --> 00:22:52.490
particularly due to what
he does with the poem.

00:22:52.490 --> 00:22:57.490
And if, as you read in
here, the poem in English

00:22:57.530 --> 00:22:59.540
you'll notice that this poem,

00:22:59.540 --> 00:23:02.230
which is dedicated to the
Letters of the Alphabet

00:23:02.230 --> 00:23:04.160
actually follows the letters

00:23:04.160 --> 00:23:06.270
of the alphabet from ATC.

00:23:06.270 --> 00:23:08.640
Each line begins with that letter

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:11.830
and also ends with that
letter and subsequent

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:14.550
and this series that follows each other.

00:23:14.550 --> 00:23:19.550
So I will read this beautiful
column in the most form.

00:23:20.850 --> 00:23:22.550
To the Letters of the Alphabet.

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:25.890
Arden's letter, your
blood will be ephemerra,

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:28.690
brief, like flowers of the baobab,

00:23:28.690 --> 00:23:32.110
creating words that other letters (sic)

00:23:32.110 --> 00:23:35.320
drop from, a ruse blundered.

00:23:35.320 --> 00:23:39.100
Elsewhere, writing was an another empire

00:23:39.100 --> 00:23:42.200
fragment, crib, knot, raga and kif.

00:23:43.590 --> 00:23:46.070
Grave, thunder of a golden gong,

00:23:46.070 --> 00:23:48.503
hint and reflection of an ancient aleph;

00:23:49.370 --> 00:23:51.903
image that the space gives of its own.

00:23:53.350 --> 00:23:56.743
Joining together letters, sun and haj,

00:23:56.743 --> 00:24:00.310
..Kafka finds himself with his double, K

00:24:00.310 --> 00:24:04.050
lackadaisical writing of
a rumor that's lethal..

00:24:04.050 --> 00:24:06.763
Hano filled, combined
in the words of lull.

00:24:07.650 --> 00:24:11.010
Maliarme doesn't forget
this, nor does Islam

00:24:11.010 --> 00:24:13.440
nor the monk who taught in the monsoon

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:16.140
or he who wrote with letters and prayed,

00:24:16.140 --> 00:24:21.140
Oh, Piet Mondrian prints
listening to be bop.

00:24:21.630 --> 00:24:24.840
Question: Who is Duchamp and who HOOq?

00:24:24.840 --> 00:24:28.090
Reflected in the trivial and the stellar

00:24:28.090 --> 00:24:32.520
signs, silences do we not see the shadow?

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:36.133
Or is there indeed all the
being of light, as in Rembrand?

00:24:37.130 --> 00:24:39.540
Universe of letters where you,

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:41.926
view painted cities Tel Aviv,

00:24:41.926 --> 00:24:45.890
Westminster by Monet, the gray Glasgow;

00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:48.053
xylography of the crude Sfix.

00:24:49.070 --> 00:24:52.470
Ye, here is signature; Severo Sarduy...

00:24:52.470 --> 00:24:56.810
Zagging, left-handed
algorithm of stubborn glitz...

00:24:56.810 --> 00:24:59.833
Geneva 8-8-88.

00:25:01.160 --> 00:25:03.810
So the final poem that I like to read

00:25:03.810 --> 00:25:06.350
come from a time when we started

00:25:06.350 --> 00:25:11.350
we was well aware of his own
eminent passing in writing

00:25:11.631 --> 00:25:15.818
epitaphs both to himself about himself,

00:25:15.818 --> 00:25:20.818
but about his friends as well,
who were also dying of AIDS.

00:25:25.148 --> 00:25:26.400
Epitaphs.

00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:27.233
One.

00:25:28.410 --> 00:25:30.950
Here lies, deaf and severe,

00:25:30.950 --> 00:25:33.700
he who used so many shoes, soles,

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:37.570
whose tips he abused from
the front and from behind.

00:25:37.570 --> 00:25:40.630
Laconic adagio... and soothsayer...

00:25:40.630 --> 00:25:42.578
Inscribe on his tomb

00:25:42.578 --> 00:25:44.902
the skeleton crumbles,

00:25:44.902 --> 00:25:47.190
gold of jewels undone

00:25:48.310 --> 00:25:50.377
his name, and between two dates,

00:25:50.377 --> 00:25:52.067
"the dead man's out to dance".

00:25:56.280 --> 00:25:57.113
Five.

00:25:58.990 --> 00:26:03.210
Death, you are so lazy in
delivering your sentence

00:26:03.210 --> 00:26:05.350
here rests your witness.

00:26:05.350 --> 00:26:07.430
It astonishes, and even delights

00:26:07.430 --> 00:26:10.400
to see you labor, to see you scarce,

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:13.540
to see your disappearance with
the pleasure of surrender,

00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:17.500
your siege of ash hounding
the funny vertebrae,

00:26:17.500 --> 00:26:18.833
that's your way of being.

00:26:21.630 --> 00:26:22.900
Six.

00:26:22.900 --> 00:26:26.380
Savage, like the lashing
of rags and putrefaction.

00:26:26.380 --> 00:26:29.170
Death's violent gob of spit.

00:26:29.170 --> 00:26:32.820
There's no embrace more
faithful or long-term

00:26:32.820 --> 00:26:36.290
two dates as Stroud of
dry and brief design

00:26:36.290 --> 00:26:38.930
my confession and abomination

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:41.700
jewel, colophon, and emblem

00:26:41.700 --> 00:26:43.313
of the buroque funeral.

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:47.963
Six.

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:53.350
At my funeral, may there be
cheese and guava and music;

00:26:53.350 --> 00:26:57.130
May the mortuary protocols
be abridged and limited.

00:26:57.130 --> 00:26:59.000
No excessive lamenting.

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:01.300
No bach, light music.

00:27:01.300 --> 00:27:03.520
Play La Sanora Matancera.

00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:05.550
For good taste, colors

00:27:05.550 --> 00:27:07.360
don't lay flowers on me

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:09.423
If, on the highway, I die.

00:27:10.610 --> 00:27:14.770
I realize there's some numbering
issues here in the edition.

00:27:14.770 --> 00:27:17.010
This was six and the final one was seven

00:27:17.010 --> 00:27:20.930
but that ends the reading.

00:27:20.930 --> 00:27:23.940
And now I'll stop sharing the screen.

00:27:23.940 --> 00:27:28.940
And I welcome conversation
questions about these poems.

00:27:30.530 --> 00:27:33.973
The process of translating
them and anything else as well.

00:27:35.930 --> 00:27:37.480
- Great, thank you so much, David.

00:27:37.480 --> 00:27:38.313
Thank you.

00:27:38.313 --> 00:27:40.559
I'll just be brief and say
thank you to the organizers

00:27:40.559 --> 00:27:43.790
of this event expedition
to all the sponsors.

00:27:43.790 --> 00:27:45.420
We really appreciated and thanks.

00:27:45.420 --> 00:27:46.900
Most of all, of course, you to the public

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:48.620
for being here via Zoom.

00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:50.550
We wish we could celebrate
this in person, of course

00:27:50.550 --> 00:27:52.270
but this is, we're
working with what we got.

00:27:52.270 --> 00:27:53.900
So, it's a real pleasure to be here

00:27:53.900 --> 00:27:56.640
with all of you and I'll just get started.

00:27:56.640 --> 00:27:57.820
I will also encourage you

00:27:57.820 --> 00:27:59.640
